WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.370
We had a fascinating interview last night
on Rachel Mattos program with Donald Trump's

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:08.480
former personal attorney, Michael Cohen.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.340
Now, I don't really watch MSNBC, CNN and Fox
News other than.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.560
Services that will sort of clip notable things
for me that I might want to talk about on

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:18.560
the program.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.560
I haven't seen Rachel Maddow.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.619
Rachel Maddow show in years.

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:25.400
I think the last time I saw it was when she
had that episode about, oh, we have Trump's

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.400
taxes.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.230
But it was like a 15 year old page, one of
a ten forty or something like that.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.670
Last night's interview that Rachel Maddow
did with Michael Cohen is fascinating.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.300
And there's a ton of great material in the
interview.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.110
I encourage you to check out the entire interview,
if it's interesting to you.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.580
But I want to focus in on one thing today,
which is Michael Cohen predicted yesterday,

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.660
something we've been talking about for a while.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.260
By we I don't mean I mean, you know, with
the audience.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:55.040
It's been in the ether for a while, which
is the idea that if Donald Trump loses to

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.590
Joe Biden in November, sometime between November
and January 20th, Donald Trump will resign

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:05.400
in order to make Mike Pence president, in
order for Mike Pence to preemptively issue

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.010
a blanket pardon for Donald Trump.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.740
We've been talking about this for a while.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.760
Callers regularly ask about it and suggest
it.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.930
Here is Michael Cohen saying he believes that
that is exactly what Donald Trump will do

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.240
if he loses to Joe Biden in November.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.340
But you said at the very end of the book that
the president and Attorney General William

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:31.440
Barr ousted the U.S. attorney in the southern
district of New York and tried to install

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.600
effectively the president's golfing buddy
as the new U.S. attorney there, because the

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.090
president, in your view, wanted to arrange
for himself to be indicted while he's still

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:47.320
in office because that would give him the
opportunity to pardon himself after he lost

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.320
the election.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.970
I just wanted to make sure that I understand
the point that you're making is to ask you

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.250
to elaborate on that a little bit.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.020
I'm not sure I understand.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:03.011
My my theory is that if he loses, there is
still the time between the election and the

00:02:03.011 --> 00:02:09.380
time that the next president will take office
and in during that time, my suspicion is that

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.390
he will resign as president.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.099
He will allow Mike Pence to take over.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:18.170
And he will then go ahead and have Mike Pence
pardon him.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:22.650
It's a very let's just say it's a very Nixon
type of event.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:30.090
And it was probably discussed between Roger
Stone and President Trump at some point.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.110
This is certainly one way to avoid any potential
prison time.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.710
So there's a few thoughts here.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.840
There's actually a big legal question here.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:46.250
And often we hear this kind of flat conventional
wisdom that pardons apply only to federal

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:47.320
prosecution.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.230
Presidential pardons apply only to federal
prosecution.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.209
But not to state prosecutions.

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:53.740
And that as such.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.060
Mike Pence can't protect Donald Trump from
state prosecution.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:06.140
The counter argument is that some believe
a blanket preemptive pardon would cover state

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.860
prosecutions.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:12.710
The counter to that would be, well, there
was the idea that that was the case with the

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.260
Richard Nixon pardons.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.819
But it was never actually challenged in court
because charges were not actually brought.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:24.849
So we don't really know there really isn't
judicial precedent in terms of the legality

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:27.650
or the reality of a preemptive pardon.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.790
Does it cover a potential state charges or
does it not?

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.250
It's a debate is the point.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:40.490
The other important counter or or factor is
when we talk about Richard Nixon's crimes,

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.430
they were much narrower in scope than all
of the different stuff that Trump has been

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.760
colloquially accused of.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.050
And they really only took place when Nixon
was president with Donald Trump.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:57.470
We're talking about myriad crimes, including
crimes allegedly committed before Donald Trump

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.620
became president anyway.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.930
And the idea would be preemptive.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:03.240
Pardon or not?

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:08.599
It's going to be much more difficult for Mike
Pence to preemptively get Trump out of trouble

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:10.209
definitively.

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:13.000
That's sort of one aspect of this.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.079
The other important aspect of this entire
discussion is that clearly the time between

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:23.400
Election Day and Inauguration Day could be
very dangerous if Donald Trump does lose.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:30.250
Now that I have been saying for a long time
and warning people about and we need to account

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:36.710
for that in thinking about how durable are
are our institutions, how prepared is Joe

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:43.410
Biden and and the Democratic Party and all
other aspects of this country to deal with

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:49.620
what might be armed Trump supporters in the
streets between November 3rd and January 20th

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.520
if Trump loses and convinces his followers
that this thing was rigged?

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.030
The most important thing here is Trump would
rather just win than to have to deal with

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:02.470
even this idea of should I resign and Pence
pardoning me.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:07.060
So because Trump would rather just win, he's
going to do anything he can.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.780
Anything he can think of to win fairly or
unfairly.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.240
And yes, our blood should be boiling about
what's going on.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.440
And yes, Donald Trump must be at least somewhat
scared of what might happen if he does lose.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.840
But he is going to try to solve a personal
problem by disenfranchizing you.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.160
That's where the Postal Service stuff comes
from.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:32.240
That's where delegitimizing the trustworthiness
of the election is coming from.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.880
That's where all of this is coming from.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:39.210
So bottom line, I can't see Donald Trump simply
accepting defeat.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:43.699
I don't imagine that if Biden wins, I was
looking at one scenario where Biden gets to

00:05:43.699 --> 00:05:46.199
eighty nine electoral.

00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:50.880
I can't imagine that with Biden at two eighty
nine, Trump just says, I concede.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:51.949
Thanks, guys.

00:05:51.949 --> 00:05:53.130
I lost.

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:57.700
I think in the event of a Biden landslide,
Trump will come up with something to say.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:02.740
He's going to start writing executive orders,
declaring the results suspect and trying to

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.070
postpone a transition.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.070
I don't know.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.669
He's at least going to say something about
it if it's close.

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:13.270
He will do everything he can to use lawyers
to stop the counting of absentee ballots.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.770
He's going to try something.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.199
I believe he will eventually go.

00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:23.479
If he loses, but he's going to try something
and we have to make sure that we are prepared

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:24.479
for it.

00:06:24.479 --> 00:06:27.889
As I said earlier, I don't know if Joe Biden
and the Democratic Party are prepared for

00:06:27.889 --> 00:06:28.889
it.

00:06:28.889 --> 00:07:37.379
I hope they are.

